WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
Hypothesis testing.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:07.767
It's the machinery behind every A/B test you'll run on models, prompts, and rerankers.

3
00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:10.633
Here's the logic of a test.

4
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:14.300
First, assume the null hypothesis: there's no difference.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.667
Collect your data.

6
00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:19.367
Ask how surprising that data would be, if the null were true.

7
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:26.200
If it's very surprising, below a threshold alpha, usually zero point zero five, you reject the null.

8
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.933
Picture all the gaps between two groups that pure noise could produce.

9
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:32.850
Most land near the centre.

10
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.400
Small gaps are easy for noise to explain.

11
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.929
Only rarely does noise create a gap out in the tails.

12
00:00:38.929 --> 00:00:41.367
If ours lands there, we reject the null.

13
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:43.600
Let's run one.

14
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.700
Old and new latencies, where version two is truly eighty milliseconds faster.

15
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.331
The t-test compares the two means.

16
00:00:51.331 --> 00:00:55.033
A small p value says reject the idea of no difference.

17
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.467
Here's a real case.

18
00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:04.000
Swap the reranker and faithfulness moves from point eight one to point eight four on sixty questions.

19
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.100
Run a paired test on the per-question scores before celebrating.

20
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.447
With noisy judged metrics, that gap often fails to clear the null.

21
00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:14.900
The test stops you shipping a coin flip.

22
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.733
A few rules keep you honest.

23
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:19.638
You never prove the null.

24
00:01:19.638 --> 00:01:21.884
You reject it or fail to reject it.

25
00:01:21.884 --> 00:01:24.900
Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.

26
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.567
Pick the test for your data: t-test for means, chi-squared for counts, Mann-Whitney for skewed data.

27
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:37.167
And choose alpha and sample size before looking, because peeking inflates false positives.

28
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.100
Let's recap.

29
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.400
Assume the null, then ask how surprising your data is.

30
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.500
Reject only when it falls below a threshold you set in advance.

31
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.283
You never prove the null, you only fail to reject it.

32
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:51.400
Next up: P-value.
